100. FINAL SUMMARY
============================================================

The most important rules to remember:

RULE 1:
C can automatically convert values between compatible types.

RULE 2:
You can explicitly request conversion:

    (type)value

RULE 3:
Integer operands usually perform integer arithmetic.

    5 / 2 → 2

RULE 4:
Cast before the operation if you want the operation to use
the converted type.

    (double)5 / 2 → 2.5

RULE 5:
Casting after an integer operation is too late.

    (double)(5 / 2) → 2.0

RULE 6:
Floating-to-integer conversion truncates toward zero when
representable.

    (int)9.99 → 9
    (int)-9.99 → -9

RULE 7:
Narrowing conversions can lose information.

RULE 8:
Floating-point values may lose precision.

RULE 9:
Signed/unsigned mixing can produce surprising results.

RULE 10:
A cast does not automatically make a conversion safe.

RULE 11:
Signed integer overflow is undefined behavior.

RULE 12:
Unsigned arithmetic wraps modulo 2^N.

RULE 13:
Use compiler warnings:

    -Wall
    -Wextra
    -Wconversion
    -Wsign-conversion

RULE 14:
Always understand the type of each operand before evaluating
a mixed-type expression.


